---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import profile from '../../data/agile-profile.json';
import t from '../../data/tariffs.json';

const OCTOPUS_REFERRAL = 'https://share.octopus.energy/lush-spark-49';
const AXLE_REFERRAL = 'https://vpp.axle.energy/landing?ref=R-QADMOHUO';

const { current: cur, planned: plan, comparison: cmp } = t;
const gbp = (n: number) => `£${Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-GB')}`;
const chosen = cmp.rows.find((r: any) => r.chosen);
const today = cmp.rows.find((r: any) => r.current);
const switchSaving = today.net - chosen.net;

const slots = profile.slots as number[];
const lo = Math.min(...slots);
const hi = Math.max(...slots);
const slotTime = (i: number) => `${String(Math.floor(i / 2)).padStart(2, '0')}:${i % 2 ? '30' : '00'}`;
// 16:00–19:00 is the evening peak the battery exists to dodge; ink it so it reads as the villain.
const isPeak = (i: number) => i >= 32 && i < 38;
const shade = (v: number) => {
  const k = (v - lo) / (hi - lo);
  if (k > 0.72) return '#5b83e6';
  if (k > 0.45) return '#9ab4f2';
  if (k > 0.22) return '#c9d8f8';
  return '#e8eefc';
};
const cheapest = slots.indexOf(lo);
const dearest = slots.indexOf(hi);

const barMax = Math.max(...cmp.rows.map((r: any) => r.net));
---

<Base
  title="The tariff"
  description="What this UK solar, battery and Predbat home actually pays for electricity today, why a plain 12-month fix is the wrong tariff once a heat pump and an EV arrive, and the modelling behind the switch to Intelligent Octopus Go."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>The tariff</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The tariff matters every bit as much as the hardware, because it decides what every stored
      kWh is actually worth. Here's the unglamorous truth: right now we're on a
      {' '}<strong>plain 12-month fix at {cur.electricity.unit_p}p</strong>, which is the wrong
      tariff for this house and about to become badly wrong. This page is what we run, what we're
      moving to, and the modelling that settled it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>What we actually run today</h2></div>
  <table class="spec">
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Import</td>
        <td>
          <strong>{cur.electricity.product}</strong> — {cur.electricity.unit_p}p/kWh flat, standing
          charge {cur.electricity.standing_p}p/day. Started {cur.electricity.started}, runs to
          {' '}{cur.electricity.ends}, £{cur.electricity.exit_fee_gbp_per_fuel} exit fee per fuel.
          <span class="mono fine"> {cur.electricity.tariff_code}</span>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Gas</td>
        <td>
          <strong>{cur.gas.product}</strong> — {cur.gas.unit_p}p/kWh, standing charge
          {' '}{cur.gas.standing_p}p/day. {cur.gas.note}
          <span class="mono fine"> {cur.gas.tariff_code}</span>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Export</td>
        <td>
          <strong>{cur.export.product}</strong> — flat {cur.export.unit_p}p/kWh, no export peaks to
          chase. Boring on purpose, because it's a guaranteed floor.
          <strong> Not paying yet</strong> — see below.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>VPP</td>
        <td>
          <strong>{cur.vpp.product}</strong> — £{cur.vpp.rate_gbp_per_kwh}/kWh with a
          £{cur.vpp.monthly_minimum_gbp}/month minimum. Settles through the smart meter, so it pays
          out even without export certification. <a href="/stats/#axle">What it's actually paid us →</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p><strong>We're still giving our export away.</strong> The panels have exported
    {' '}<strong>{cur.export.kwh_given_away.toLocaleString('en-GB')} kWh</strong> since
    {' '}{cur.export.since} and earned <strong>£0</strong> for it, because the export MPAN still
    isn't registered. At {cur.export.unit_p}p that's <strong>{gbp(cur.export.value_forgone_gbp)}</strong>
    handed to the grid for nothing. The <a href="/stats/">stats</a> honestly report £0 export
    revenue throughout. If you take one thing from this site: chase the paperwork before you chase
    the optimisation.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note quiet">
    <p><strong>VAT on electricity drops from 5% to 0% on 1 October 2026.</strong> Every rate on this
    page falls about 4.8% automatically. It applies to all tariffs equally, so it changes no
    comparison — but it does mean the figures above are the last of the VAT-inclusive ones.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Why a fix stops working</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    A flat {cur.electricity.unit_p}p is perfectly reasonable for a house that just uses
    electricity. It's a poor fit for one with a battery, and a bad one for a house about to add
    {' '}<a href="/ev/">an EV</a> and <a href="/heat-pump/">a heat pump</a>. A flat rate pays the
    same whenever you use power, which means a battery has nothing to arbitrage and a heat pump has
    no cheap hours to hide in. Every unit of new demand arrives at full price.
  </p>

  <div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h2>Modelled annual cost, heat pump and car both running</h2>
      <span class="meta">export at 12p · electricity VAT at 0%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="tar-bars">
      {cmp.rows.map((r: any) => (
        <div class="trow">
          <span class="tlabel">
            {r.chosen || r.current ? <strong>{r.name}</strong> : r.name}
            <span class="tverdict">{r.verdict}</span>
          </span>
          <span class="ttrack">
            <span
              class={`tfill ${r.chosen ? 'accent' : r.current ? 'dark' : 'mid'}`}
              style={`width:${(r.net / barMax) * 100}%`}
            ></span>
          </span>
          <span class="tval">{gbp(r.net)}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      Doing nothing costs <strong>{gbp(switchSaving)} a year</strong> more than switching. The
      blended import price falls from {today.blended_p}p to {chosen.blended_p}p, because a battery
      and a six-hour cheap window between them move most of the year's demand off the day rate.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Where we're going: {plan.product}</h2></div>
  <table class="spec">
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Night</td><td><strong>{plan.night_p}p/kWh</strong>, {plan.night_window}, whole home — not just the car. Plus smart dispatch slots outside the window when the grid is generous.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Day</td><td>{plan.day_p}p/kWh. Punishing, and the whole game is never using it. The battery and the heat pump's thermal store exist to make that possible.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Standing</td><td>{plan.standing_p}p/day.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deadline</td><td>Switched <strong>{plan.deadline}</strong>. Waiting for the fix to expire in March 2027 would put an entire heating season on the wrong tariff.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Agile came out cheaper, and we're not taking it.</strong> On a full year of real
    published prices Agile models {gbp(chosen.net - cmp.rows[0].net)} a year better than
    Intelligent Octopus Go. That margin is inside the error bars: it rests on last year's price
    shape, Agile's standing charge is higher, and Octopus is currently only offering fixed products
    while citing volatility. A guaranteed {plan.night_p}p for six hours is worth more to us than a
    modelled {gbp(chosen.net - cmp.rows[0].net)} — especially with a heat pump arriving that has to
    get through January whatever the wholesale market does.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note quiet">
    <p><strong>Cosy Octopus is the designated heat pump tariff, and it finishes fourth.</strong> Its
    off-peak is around 14.5p against IOG's 8p, and its 16:00–19:00 peak runs above 50p — which is
    exactly when a heat pump wants to run on a cold evening. Cosy sells flexibility that a 15 kWh
    battery already provides. If you have a battery, check the sum before assuming the heat pump
    tariff is for you.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="cols-2" style="margin-top:1rem">
    <div class="card referral">
      <p class="kicker">Thinking of switching?</p>
      <h2 style="margin:0.2rem 0 0"><img src="/octopus-mark.png" alt="" class="octopus-logo-inline" /> Octopus · we both get £50</h2>
      <p>No sponsors on this site. The referral links are the only money it makes, and they pay you
      exactly what they pay us. You don't need solar — any tariff counts.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn octopus" href={OCTOPUS_REFERRAL} rel="noopener"><img src="/octopus-mark.png" alt="Octopus Energy" class="octopus-mark" /> Claim £50</a>
        <p class="fine mono">share.octopus.energy/lush-spark-49</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card referral">
      <p class="kicker">Got a battery already?</p>
      <h2 style="margin:0.2rem 0 0"><img src="/axle-logo-black.png" alt="" class="axle-logo-inline" /> Axle · we both get £25</h2>
      <p>Paid per grid-balancing event at £1/kWh, £10 a month guaranteed. Events-only mode keeps
      Predbat in charge the rest of the time. <a href="/stats/#axle">What it's actually paid us →</a></p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn axle" href={AXLE_REFERRAL} rel="noopener"><img src="/axle-logo-black.png" alt="Axle" class="axle-mark" /> Claim £25</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>The half-hourly price we modelled against</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    We don't run Agile, but a full year of its published half-hourly rates is the best free dataset
    there is for understanding when electricity is actually expensive — and it's what every
    comparison above was built on.
  </p>

  <section class="card">
    <div class="card-head">
      <h2>A typical day's Agile import price</h2>
      <span class="meta">mean of a full year of published rates · Jun 2025 → Jun 2026</span>
    </div>
    <div class="price-curve">
      {slots.map((v, i) => (
        <span
          class="bar"
          style={`height:${((v - lo * 0.72) / (hi - lo * 0.72)) * 100}%; background:${isPeak(i) ? 'var(--dark)' : shade(v)}`}
          title={`${slotTime(i)} — ${v.toFixed(2)}p`}
        ></span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="price-axis"><span>00:00</span><span>06:00</span><span>12:00</span><span>18:00</span><span>23:30</span></div>
    <p class="caption">
      The shape is the point: a battery charges in the pale hours and carries the house through the
      near-black 16:00–19:00 peak. Averaged over a full year the cheapest half hour is
      <strong>{lo.toFixed(2)}p at {slotTime(cheapest)}</strong> and the dearest
      <strong>{hi.toFixed(2)}p at {slotTime(dearest)}</strong>, with a mean of
      {' '}<strong>{profile.mean_p_per_kwh}p</strong>. Intelligent Octopus Go buys that same cheap
      window at a guaranteed price instead of a forecast one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>The Flux plot twist</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    The original business case was built on <strong>Octopus Flux</strong>, the classic battery
    tariff with a cheap overnight window and a 30p evening export peak. The plan was dead simple:
    charge cheap overnight, force-export the battery into the 4pm to 7pm peak, profit. Then in
    <strong>March 2026</strong> Flux and Intelligent Flux closed to new customers, months before
    our panels went on the roof. Standard Flux has since reopened; Intelligent Flux was still shut
    as of August 2026.
  </p>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p><strong>Lesson one:</strong> don't pin a multi-year payback sum on one tariff sticking
    around. Tariffs are products, and products get pulled. Model your second-best option too. We
    hadn't, and re-planning under time pressure was no fun at all.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">
    Here's the surprise: losing Flux's 30p export peak cost far less than expected, because the
    battery's job simply flips from <em>exporting at a peak rate</em> to <em>not importing at a
    peak rate</em>, and dodging expensive evening import is worth roughly as much. With a heat pump
    and a car arriving, that's now emphatically the better job for it — which is why Flux stays
    ruled out even though it has reopened. Its cheap import window is about three hours, and three
    hours cannot feed a 45 kWh winter day plus a car.
  </p>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Why we keep the boring 12p export</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    Agile Outgoing does half-hourly export pricing, uncapped, with no exit fees. We modelled a full
    year of real rates against our own generation: flat 12p wins by around £110 a year. Only the
    16:00–19:00 window beats 12p, while this high-generation, low-consumption house floods the grid
    at midday when Agile export pays 5–7p. The heat pump makes that verdict stronger, not weaker —
    it eats the shoulder-season surplus that Agile Outgoing pays best for, leaving the midday
    summer spill it pays worst for.
  </p>

  <div class="note quiet">
    <p><strong>The discharge hierarchy that falls out of all this:</strong> Axle VPP at £1/kWh
    beats self-consumption at the avoided import rate, which beats exporting at 12p. Predbat is
    told as much, and it reorganises the day accordingly. The one counter-intuitive consequence:
    once we're on {plan.product} at {plan.night_p}p, cheap imported power is cheaper than our own
    solar's 12p opportunity cost — so the right move is to <em>sell</em> the sunshine and charge
    the car overnight. See <a href="/ev/">the car</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <style>
    .price-curve {
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-end;
      gap: 2px;
      height: 190px;
      margin-top: 0.5rem;
    }
    .price-curve .bar { flex: 1; border-radius: 2px 2px 0 0; min-height: 3px; }
    .price-axis {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      color: var(--ink-400);
      margin-top: 0.4rem;
    }
    .tar-bars { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.6rem; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
    .trow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 15rem 1fr 4rem; align-items: center; gap: 0.8rem; }
    .tlabel { font-size: 0.86rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
    .tverdict { font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--ink-400); }
    .ttrack { background: var(--track); border-radius: 999px; height: 12px; overflow: hidden; }
    .tfill { display: block; height: 100%; border-radius: 999px; min-width: 6px; background: #9ab4f2; }
    .tfill.accent { background: var(--accent); }
    .tfill.dark { background: var(--dark); }
    .tfill.mid { background: #9ab4f2; }
    .tval { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.78rem; text-align: right; color: var(--ink-400); }
    @media (max-width: 620px) {
      .trow { grid-template-columns: 1fr 4rem; }
      .tlabel { grid-column: 1 / -1; margin-bottom: -0.3rem; }
    }
  </style>
</Base>
